Doug Freyburger wrote:


> Once software reaches maturity it's release cycle is much slower and it
> continues to get slower over time.  It's natural for any software.  For
> any one software product (a technical term that doesn't work great in
> the freeware realm) there's a maximum it will ever reach and once it
> gets close it switches from an exponential growth curve to an asymptotic
> approach curve.  It's called the "S shaped curve" by most.
 
> NetHack has been declared mature code according to its sourceforge page.

I tend to agree.  Nethack has fulfilled the potential its basic design and 
data structures made possible.  

Fundamental advances in the game, at this point, would require a different 
underlying architecture, design, and data structures.  It's reached the
point where the only things you can really change, without ripping a bunch 
of stuff out and rewriting it according to a radically different software
design, are trivial details.  

There's definitely a bugfix release to be made.  But beyond that, minor 
reshuffling of details is all that's possible within the identity of the 
current game.  If enough basic architecture to change something major 
gets changed, the game won't be nethack anymore.  

People who think Nethack needs a new release are invited to play Rogue.

